WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.096
Lukas: A concurrent index build still takes locks. It can wait for older transactions.

2
00:00:06.096 --> 00:00:12.600
Raj: So a lock wait remains possible. Ordinary writes can continue through most phases.

3
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:18.912
Anika: I said lock-free earlier, that's wrong. I meant reduced blocking for this engine.

4
00:00:18.912 --> 00:00:26.904
Priya: Just to confirm, engine-specific behavior, right? MySQL online DDL has different limits.

5
00:00:26.904 --> 00:00:36.000
Hannah: Exactly, including metadata locks and algorithms. Don't copy this approval across databases.

6
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:43.320
Raj: The statement runs outside transaction blocks. We'll configure bounded waits through reviewed settings.

7
00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:50.208
Lukas: Define a rollback trigger before execution. Cancellation may leave an unusable index.

8
00:00:50.208 --> 00:00:58.224
Priya: Okay, simultaneous schema changes? Let's park that. Maybe they compete for table locks.

9
00:00:58.224 --> 00:01:06.144
Hannah: Our working hypothesis isn't zero impact. We expect tolerable waits with monitoring.

10
00:01:06.144 --> 00:01:12.120
Anika: So, I'll correct the change description. It must state that risk clearly.

